full_judgment:
Mamnoon Rahman J: The First Appeal No. 229 of 2008, Civil Rule No. 362(F)/2010 as well as Civil Rule No. 529(R)/2010 are disposed of herewith by a common judgment as they do involve similar question of fact and law and the parties are same.

2. This appeal being First Appeal No.229 of 2008 is directed against the judgment and decree dated 10-8-2008 (decree drawn on 181-1 2008) passed by the Joint District Judge, 7th Court, Dhaka in Title Suit No.958 of 2008.

3. During pendency of the appeal the appellant preferred an application for injunction resulting which this Court vide order dated 31-5-2010 issued Rule upon the opposite party to show cause as to why the opposite party should not be restrained vide order of injunction as prayed for as well as this Court at the time of issuance of the Rule, directed the parties to maintain status quo in respect of possession of the suit property for a period of 8 weeks.

4. Subsequently son an application filed by the appellant petitioner under Order XXXIX, rule 2(3) read with section 151 of the Code of Civil procedure another Rule was issued calling upon the opposite party to show cause as to why proceeding should not be drawn for violation of the order passed by this Court on 31-5-2010.

5. The appellant, as plaintiffs, instituted Title Suit being No. 958 of 2008 in the Court of Joint District Judge, 7th Court, Dhaka for partition along with a prayer for declaration that the deed being No. 6391 dated 11-10-1968 is forged, fraudulent, ineffective and not binding upon the plaintiff. The case of the plaintiffs in short is that the original owner and possessor of the suit land was Sekina Begum, maternal grand mother of the plaintiff petitioner and respondent Nos. 2 and 3 and the mother of respondent No.1, who purchased the said land vide registered deed No. 3427 dated 4-7-1947 from one Shaimaty Shantipoda Debi. Shakina Begum was owning and possessing the said land including pond and building with an area of 0.65 acres of land. She died leaving behind plaintiff and defendant No. 1 and also defendant Nos. 2 and 3 as her legal heirs. The said property was not partitioned amongst the said heirs, and now the heirs of the late Shakina Begum have been owning and possessing the said land as ejmali property. The defendant No. 1 is the son of Sakina Bibi and defendant No. 3 is the son of Begum Sayeda. Begum Sayeda died leaving behind one son Md Farook, the plaintiff petitioner and defendant Nos. 2 and 3 and the suit properties of Begum Sayeda i.e. all the heirs have been possessing the said land at their sweet will and the plaintiff-petitioner has been possessing a small part of the suit land and the rest of the land has been in possession of the defendant respondent opposite parties which are more land than that of the plaintiff-petitioner land, the plaintiff-petitioner is the grandson (Nati) of late Shakina Begum who will get 2/12 shares amounting to 1083 ajutangsha, and on the other hand defendant Nos. 2 and 3 would get 1083 ajutangsha, the plaintiff petitioner asked the defendant-opposite party No.1 Serajul Islam to give their share but on reply he stated that they will not get any share of the suit land as the mother of the defendant No. 1 Shakina Begum gifted all the property of the suit land in favour of defendant opposite party No. 1 and after hearing this the plaintiff-petitioner on 21-1-2008 found out that he created a deed by false personification which was clearly described in the schedule of kha and the said deed was created and the mother of the defendant-opposite party No. 1 was not owning of the said land as the owner and possessor of the said land was Abdul Matin Dhali. The husband of Shakina Begum and their heirs was alive while the deed was created by the defendant-respondent opposite party No.1 which are false, fabricated and created by false personification and while the said deed was created by false personification and while the said deed was created the defendant-opposite party No. 1 was in France. So the possession of the said property was not at all handed over to him which was the main ingredient of a deed of heba-bil-ewaz which was not at all complied with. Moreso, the owner and possessor of the property mentioned in the schedule Mosammat Shakina Begum purchased the said by a registered deed being No. 3427 dated 4-7-1949 and the suit land was an ejmali property which was never partitioned between the heirs of Mosammat Shakina Begum by mates and bounds as a result the plaintiff-petitioner is unable to develop the said property. The cause of action firstly arose on 10-1-2008 when the defendant denied to partition the said property for which the plaintiff-petitioner was compelled to file the suit for partition with declaration that the deed in question is not binding upon the plaintiff as it is false and concocted to the said deed.

6. The defendant opposite party No. 1 contested the suit by filing written statement denying all the material allegation made in the plaint. The defendant in their written statement categorically denied the case of the plaintiffs in 19 paragraphs as well as stated their case in a detailed manner.

7. Thereafter, the defendant-respondents filed an application under Order VII, rule 11, (A) to (D) for rejection of the plaint. The same was contested by the plaintiffs appellant by way of written objection. The Court below after considering the facts and circumstances, provision of law, the said impugned judgment and decree rejected the plaint. Being aggrieved the plaintiffs appellant moved before this Court by way of appeal and consequently obtained subsequent two Rules one relating to injunction and one relating to violation of the order passed by this Court.

8. Mr Md Ibrahim Mollah, the learned Advocate appearing on behalf of the appellant submits that the Court below without applying its judicial mind and without considering the facts and circumstances most illegally and in an arbitrary manner passed the impugned judgment and decree and thereby the Court below committed an error which requires interference by his Court. He submits that Order VII, rule 11 of the Code of Civil procedure deals with the provision relating to rejection of plaint and admittedly the present case in hand does not come within the purview of the conditions as stipulated in Order VII, rule 11 of the Code as such, the judgment and decree passed by the Court below is liable to be set-aside for ends of justice. He submits that while rejecting the plaint the Court below not only considered the plaint in its entirety but also considered the defence case as narrated by the defendant in their written statement and, as such, the Court below violated the principle relates to rejection of plaint which tantamount to miscarriage of justice. He submits that it is now a well settled proposition of law that while rejecting the plaint the Court below is to satisfy itself that on the assertion available or apparent on the face of the plaint is sufficient to reject the plaint but in the case in hand while rejecting the plaint the Court below considered the question of limitation, res judicata and other issues in a detailed manner which can only be done after framing specific issues on that point. He submits that in the instant case in hand the Court below not only travelled beyond the plaint but also considered written statement filed by the defendant in a detailed manner as well as while rejecting the plaint the Court below upon his own findings and some hypothecation rejected the plaint which is liable to be set-aside for ends of justice. He submits that it is now well settled by this Court as well as of our Apex Court that the question of limitation as well as the question of res judicata are mixed questions of fact and law and these questions can only be resolved after framing issues on the particular points and in such circumstances, the impugned order so far it relates to rejection of plaint not only causes injustice but also non suited the plaintiff from claiming their legal right in the Court of law. In support of their contention the learned Advocate for the appellant relied on the decisions reported in 50 DLR (AD) 99, 49 DLR (1997) 531, 49 DLR (1997) 234, 50 DLR (1998) 29, 52 DLR (2000) 194. The learned Advocate further submits that despite an order of injunction by way of status quo granted by this Court on 31-5-2010 the defendant respondent opposite party violated the order passed by this Hon'ble Court for which this Court issued Rule and on the proven facts and circumstances an appropriate order is also required under Order XXXIX, rule 2(3), of the Code of Civil Procedure. In support of his contention, the learned Advocate placed the application for drawing proceeding Annexure-B to show the manner of violation in the present case in hand.

9. Mr AK Rashedul Huq, the learned Advocate appearing on behalf of the respondents opposite parties vehemently opposes the appeal as well as Rules. He submits that the, Court below on proper appreciation of the facts and circumstances and the provision of law has rightly rejected the plaint and thereby the same requires no interference by this Court. The learned Advocate referred the plaint as well as the written statement and submits that while rejecting the plaint the Court below came to a conclusion regarding the question of limitation in a lawful manner as well as addressed the other issues which are very much legal and acceptable as well as applicable for 'invoking the provision laid down in Order VII, rule 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure. He submits that apart from the provision as laid down in Order VII, rule 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure a Court of law exercising the power conferred under section 151 of the Code can also reject a plaint: if the same is not covered within the ground as stipulated in Order VII, rule 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure. He submits that exercising the inherent power the Court of law reserved the absolute power to reject a plaint if it is apparent that the further proceeding of a suit is fruitless. The learned Advocate placed certain facts and circumstances and submits that there are serious suppression 'of fad and declaration as sought relates to. the deed which is a very old one as well as the old description to the suit property was not there though the plaintiffs is praying for decree of partition at well as there are certain admissions by the plaintiff in the plaint regarding the Heba-bil-ewaz. He submits that the present case in hand not only is hit by the provision 'Order VII, rule 11(D) of the Code but also attract the provision of section 151 of the Code as it is apparent on the face of the record of the instant suit is nothing but a fruitless litigation and harassing the parties for indefinite period. He Submits that the plaintiff failed to proof their claim in the plaint in an appropriate manner rather the plaint was not drawn in accordance with the, provision of the Code of Civil Procedure as the grounds as pressed and stated by the Court below while rejecting the plaint are fit and proper ground as such, the impugned judgment and decree is liable to be sustained for ends of justice. He submits that it is apparent from the plaint that there is no cause of action rather the plaintiffs failed to make out any prima-facie case regarding the question of limitation, cause Of action as well as other issues and, as such, the trial Court committed no illegality in rejecting the, plaint. The learned Advocate also submits that apart from invocation of section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure, the Court of law while rejecting the plaint can travel beyond the plaint to ascertain documents and materials which are admitted in the plaint itself and, as such, there is no harm in relying upon the documents and materials on record beyond the plaint which are admitted by the parties in the plaint and in the present case in hand the other materials which includes written statement filed by the defendants are admitted by both the parties and, as such, the Court below committed no illegality in rejecting the plaint summarily. Regarding injunction he submits that since the plaint was rejected as such, the appellant plaintiffs are not entitled to get any injunction as much as there was no violation as because the said show cause was in existence much earlier before filing of the suit in hand. In support of his contention the learned Advocate also relied upon the decision reported 50 DLR (AD) 99. He also referred the decisions reported in 35 DLR 39, PLR (2008) (SC) 371, 51 DLR (AD) 155, 49 DLR (AD) 151, 54 DLR 310, 22 BLD (HCD) 423, 2002 (SCR) 338 and 53 DLR (AD) 12.

10. We have perused the impugned judgment and decree, Memorandum of Appeal, counter-affidavits, application for injunction, application for drawing up proceeding under Order XXXIX, rules 2 and 3 of the Code and other orders passed by this Court. We have also perused the provisions of law and decisions as referred to. Admittedly the plaintiffs appellant filed a suit being No.958 of 2008 in the Court of Joint District Judge, Dhaka for the following reliefs:

১৩। বাদীর প্রার্থনা এই যে,

(ক) নালিশী ক তফসিলে বর্ণিত ২/১২ অংশে ১০৮৩ অযুতাংশ সম্পত্তি বাদীর বরাবরে এক সুনিদিষ্ট ছাহাম বন্টনের প্রাথমিক ডিক্রি দিতে;

(খ) খ তফসিল বর্ণিত বিগত ১১-১০-৮৬ ইং তারিখ এর রেজিস্ট্রাকৃত ৬৩৯১ নং হেবা দলিল তঞ্চকী অবৈধ যোগসাজশী অকার্য্কর ও বাদীর উপর বাধ্য কর নহে মর্মে ঘোষনা মূলক ডিক্রি দিতে;

(গ) ক প্রাথর্না মোতাবেক প্রাথমিক ডিক্রির পর বিজ্ঞ আদালত কর্তৃক একজন কমিশনার নিয়োগ করিয়া নালিশী সম্পত্তিতে বাদীর ছাহাম চিহ্নিত করিয়া সকল  প্রকার বাধা বিঘ্ন অপসারণক্রমে দখল বুঝাইয়া দিয়া বিজ্ঞ আদালতে প্রতিবেদন দাখিল পূবর্ক চুড়ান্ত ডিক্রি প্রচার করিতে;

(ঘ) মোকদ্দমার সম্যক ব্যয়ের ডিক্রি দিতে;

(ঙ) আইন ও ইকুইটি মূলে বাদী আর যে সকল প্রতিকার পাইতে হকদার তৎমর্মে ডিক্রি দিতে সদয় মর্জি হয়।

11. It appears that after filing of the suit, notices were served upon the defendants and the defendant entered appearance and filed written statement in a detailed manner on 29-5-2008 thereafter the defendants application under Order VII, rule 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure on 8-7-2008. The main contention of the defendants is that the plaintiffs by suppression of earlier knowledge of facts instituted the suit showing 10-1-2008 and 21-1-2008 as the cause of action which is not correct and, as such, there is no cause of action for the instituted suit in hand. The further contention is that since the deed in question was executed 40 years back and the same was within the knowledge of the plaintiffs and the predecessor in interest as such, the same is hit by the question of limitation. The further case as stated in the application is that neither the mother of the plaintiffs nor her other co-sharer claimed the-suit property in any manner and since the defendants predecessor executed the deed in the year 1968, the right and title of the donor was relinquished as the same is vested upon the defendant No.1 and, as such, the plaint is liable to be rejected. It appears that the same was contested by the appellant by way of written objection. The Court below vide the impugned judgment and decree rejected the plaint.

12. Admittedly the suit is for partition as well as declaration to the effect that a deed is not binding upon the plaintiffs as much as fraudulent and collusive one. It further appears from the lower Court record that the defendants before filing of the application under Order VII, rule 11 of the Code filed detailed written statement denying all the material allegation made by the plaintiffs as, much as, in the written statement the defendant, narrated their case in a detailed manner. Order VII, rule 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure deals with the provision relates to the rejection of plaint which runs as follows;

The plaint shall be rejected in the following case:

(a) Where it does not disclose a cause of action;

(b) Where the relief claimed is undervalued, and the plaintiff on being required by the Court to correct the valuation within a time to be fixed by the Court, fails to do so;

(c) Where the relief claimed is properly valued, but the plaint is written upon a paper insufficiently stamped, and the plaintiff on being required by the Court to supply the requisite stamp-paper within a time to be fixed by the Court, fails to do so:

(d) Where the suit appears form the statement in the plaint to be barred by any law;

(e) Where any of the provisions of rule 9(IA) is not complied with and the plaintiff on being required by the Court to comply therewith within a time to be fixed by the Court fails to do so [Provided that the time fixed by the Court for the correction of the valuation or supplying the requisite stamp-paper shall not exceed twenty-one days.]

13. As per the said provision of law, it appears that a suit which is on the face of it is not competent under the law should not be allowed to proceed. A plaint can be rejected only on the grounds stated in the Rule and in the absence of any such ground the question of maintainability can be gone into at the time of hearing of the suit. There is no legal scope to reject the plaint unless the plaint itself shows the want of cause of action or the suit is barred by law. In determining whether a plaint is to be rejected, the court will take only the plaint and documents filed therewith into consideration and not what has been urged by the defendants in a petition (4 in the written statement. The power to reject a plaint or to return it for amendment should not be exercised, except in a clear case. Where on the averments made in the no cause of action is disclosed, the court is bound to reject the plaint. For ascertainment of cause of action the court has to read the plaint in its entirety.

14. Where materials other than the plaint has to be looked into to decide whether a suit is barred by law. The plaint cannot be rejected, plea of implied bar has to be decided on taking evidence unless the bar is patent. Where the suit on the averments made in the plaint appears to be barred by limitation, the plaint has to be rejected, but it is not mandatory and the court may allow the plaintiff to amend the plaint so as to enable the plaintiff to aver facts which would render the suit not to be barred limitation. However, a plaint cannot be rejected on the ground of limitation when questions of fact are involved. The rule is not exhaustive in the matter of rejection of plaint, in exceptional situation a plaint can also be rejected under section 151 of the Code even if it does not come within the mischief of this rule. A suit may be specifically barred by law in which case this rule is attracted. But even in a case where a suit is not permitted by necessary implication of law in the sense that a positive prohibition can be spelt out of the legal provision, the court has inherent jurisdiction to reject a plaint. A plaint may be rejected when there is an implied bar. However, the plea of implied bar should ordinarily be decided on evidence unless, the facts disclosed in the plaint clearly show that the suit is not maintainable. When the ultimate result of the suit is as clear as day light, such a suit should be buried at its inception so that no further time is consumed in fruitless litigation.

15. It appears that while rejecting the plaint in hand the Court below basically came to a conclusion that there is no cause of action as well as the suit is barred by limitation. While passing the impugned judgment and decree the Court below came to a conclusion that the prayer or the claim relates to partition is relied upon the decisions regarding the deed in question executed in the year 1968 and as per the Court below if the plaintiffs fail to prove the claim so far it relates to the deed in question they are not entitled to get a decree in partition. It appears that the Court below held as follows;

বাদীপক্ষ দাখিলী আরজীর কোথাও বিগত ১১-১০-৬৮ ইং তারিখের ৬৩৯১ নং দলিলে দলিল দাত্রী সখিনা থাতুনের স্বাক্ষর নাই মর্মে সুস্পষ্ট ভাবে উল্লেখ করেন নাই ৷ সখিনা খাতুনের পরিবর্তে অন্য ব্যক্তিকে সখিনা খাতুন হিসাবে দাঁড় করিয়া উক্ত দলিল খানা সৃজন করা হইয়াছে মর্মে দাবী করেন নাই ৷

16. The Court further held:

বাদীপক্ষ আরজীতে বলিয়াছেন যে, সখিনা খাতুন স্বামী-মাতা, কন্যাকে. বঞ্চিত করিয়া কেবল মাত্র ১নং বিবাদীকেই সমস্ত সম্পত্তি দিয়াছিলেন, ইহা ও সম্পূর্ণ মিথ্যা, জাল, জালিয়াতি ও তঞ্চকী ৷ কিন্তু বাদীপক্ষের এই বক্তব্য আদৌ আমল যোগ্য নয়্।

17. So it appears that thought the plaintiffs claimed that the said deed, was executed by depriving other heirs which cannot be accepted as per the Court. In such circumstances, the court held as follows:

কেননা আইন অনুযায়ী কোন সম্পত্তির সত্বাধিকারী তার অন্যান্য সকল আপন জনকে বঞ্চিত করিয়া নিজ সন্তানকে তার মালিকী সম্পত্তি হস্তান্তর করিয়া দিতে পারেন উহাতে কোন আইনগত বাধা নাই ৷

18. The Court further held as follows:

বাদীপক্ষ দাখিলী আরজীর ৮ম পৃষ্ঠার ৯ম অনুচ্ছেদের দ্বাদশ হইতে ত্রয়োদশ লাইনে উল্লেখ করেন যে, “যদি তিনি প্রকৃত পক্ষে হেবা করে দেন তাহা হলে খুবই গোপনে করেছেন” ৷ দাত্রী নালিশা তপসিল বর্ণিত সম্পত্তি নালিশী দলিল মূলে হেবা করিছিলেন এই তথ্যই (sic) এই বক্তব্য হতে পরিম্ফটিত হয় ৷ উল্লেখ্য , ১৯৬৮ ইং সনে ঢাকার সম্পত্তি  মুন্সেগঞ্জে রােজান্ত্র হইতে কোন আইনগত বাধা ছিলনা বিধায় মুন্সিগঞ্জে রেজিট্রি হওয়ার অজুহাতে নালিশী দলিলটিকে প্রথম দৃষ্টিতেইঁ সন্দেহের চোখে দেখা যায় না ৷

19. The Court further held as follows:

কাজেই মৃত্যুর পূর্ব পর্যন্ত নালিশী দলিলের দাত্রী নালিশী দলিলে বর্ণিত নালিশী ভূমিতে দখলে ছিলেন বিধায় নালিশী দলিল বাতিল যোগ্য মর্মে বাদীপক্ষের দাবীকে স্বীকৃতি প্রদান করা যায়না ৷ বাদীপক্ষ আরজীতে আরো বলেন ১ ১ -১০-৬৮ইং তারিখে ১নং বিবাদী সিরাজুল ইসলাম সূদূর ফ্রান্সে ছিলেন ৷ তিনি তখন সম্পত্তির দখল বুঝিয়া নিতে পারেন নাই এবং তাহার পক্ষে সম্পত্তির দখল কেহ বুঝিয়া নিয়াছে তাহার ও কোন প্রমান নাই ৷ কিন্তু সুদূর ৩৯ বৎসর ৯ মাস ২৯ দিন পূর্বের বিগত ১ ১-১০-৬৮ইং দিনটিতে ১নং বিবাদী অবশ্যই ফ্রান্সে ছিলেন মর্মে কোন তথ্যের উপর ভিত্তি করিয়া বাদীপক্ষ স্থির সিদ্ধান্তে উপনিত হইয়াছে তাহা সুস্পষ্টভাবে আরজীতে বাদীপক্ষ বলেন নাই কাজেই বাদীপক্ষে এ দাবীটিও আপাততঃ যুত্তিগ্রাহ্য ও প্রমাণিত নয় ৷

20. The Court further held as follows; -

বাদীপক্ষ আরজীতে নালিশী ভূমির আর, এস জরীপি পরিচিতি প্রদান করেননি ৷ এই মর্মে সুস্পভাবে আরজিতে বলেননি যে, নালিশী ভূমি বাবদ সর্বশেষ জরীপি খতিয়ান বাদীপক্ষের বায়া সখিনা খাতুনের নামে বা উভয় পক্ষের নামে প্রচারিত হইয়াছে এবং নালিশী ভূমির খাজনা অদ্যাবধি সখিনা খাতুনের নামে অথবা সখিনা খাতুনের মৃত্যুতে তদ্বীয় ওয়ারিশদের নামে বা উভয় পক্ষের মৌরযের নামে এজমালিতে প্রদান করা হইয়া থাকে ৷ কাজেই এই মর্মে সিদ্ধান্ত গ্রহণ করা যায় যে, নালিশীদান পত্র সম্পাদন হইবড়ার পর হইতে নালিশী ভূমির খাজনাদী এজমালিতে বা উভয় পক্ষের মৌরযের নামে আদায় না হইয়া কেবলমাত্র নালিশী দানপত্র গ্রহীতার নামেই আদায় করা হইতেছিল এবং নালিশী ভূমি বাবদ সর্বশেষ জরীপি খতিয়ান দান পত্র গ্রহীতার নামে প্রচারিত হইয়াছে এবং এই জন্যই বাদীপক্ষ আরজিতে এতদসংক্রাম্ভ কোন বাক্য ব্যয় করেননি এবং নালিশী দলিলখানা সর্বত্র ভাবে একটি কার্য্যকর দলিল এবং নালিশী  দলিলের অর্ন্তভুক্ত ভূমি বর্তমানে উক্ত দলিলের গ্রহীতার ব্যক্তিগত ভূমি উহা উভয়পক্ষের এজমালি ভূমি নয় বাদীপক্ষ আরজীর ৭ম পৃষ্ঠার ৮ম অনুচ্ছেদে উল্লেখ্য করেন যে, নালিশী দলিল সম্পাদন কালীন দলিলের দাত্রীর স্বামী, মাতা, কন্যা সকলেই জীবিত ছিলেন ৷ বাদীর আরজীর এই বক্তব্য এবং উপরিঊক্ত আলোচনার আলোকে বলা যায় যে, নালিশী দলিল খানা এই সকল আপনজনের জ্ঞাতসারে সখিনা বেগম স্বীয় ইচ্ছার প্রতিফলন স্বরূপ সম্পাদন ও রেজিট্রি করিয়াছেন বিধায়ই এবং দলিলখানা উপরিউক্ত রূপে একটি কার্যকর দলিল হওয়ায় এই সকল নিকট জন দলিল খানার বিরুদ্ধে কোন আপত্তি উত্থাপন করেননি এবং বাদীপক্ষ মৌরষানুক্রমে পূর্বাপর জ্ঞাত থাকিয়া সম্পুর্ন ৩য় পক্ষ হিসাবে কার্যকর দলিল খানার বিরূদ্ধে অত্র মোকদ্দমায় রুজু করিয়াছেন দলিলের রেজিষ্ট্রি হইবার ৩৯ বৎসর ৯মাস ১৩ দিন পর যখন উক্ত দলিলের দাত্রী সখিনা বেগম এবং বাদীপক্ষের নিকটবর্তী মৌরষ বাদীপক্ষে মাতা সায়েদা বেগম মৃত।

21. By the aforesaid findings the Court below came to a conclusion that the claim of the plaintiff are not sustainable and there is no cause 'of action as much as the plaintiffs failed to prove their prima-facie right and title. Admittedly in the decisions as referred by the learned Advocate for the appellant this Court as well as our Apex Court came to a conclusion that the question of limitation can only be considered if there is no factual aspect available. As per their Lordships when the question of limitation about such facts that should be disposed of by taking evidence after framing issues. Also in the said decisions the High Court Division came to a conclusion that the Court should look into the averments made in the plaint and cannot go beyond same to find whether the plaint is liable to be rejected or not. As much as while considering the application for rejection of plaint there is no scope to consider the possible defence plea: Apart from that the Court further came to a conclusion that the plaint 'could be rejected 'only when the Court comes to the conclusion that even if all the averments made in the plaint are taken to be proved still that the plaintiffs could not be entitled to any relief at all. Also this Court as well as of our apex Court came to a conclusion that the question of limitation and the question of resjudicata and Others issues as raised in the application for rejection of the plaint are mixed questions of fact and law which needs through investigation on the basis of credible evidence for arriving in a correct decision by framing specific issues. In the case reported in 35 DLR 39 the Court below' deals with, the question of limitation but the same does not relate to rejection of plaint rather While deciding the judgment and order the Court came to a conclusion regarding the question of limitation in that case. In the case reported in PLD (2008) (SC) 31 the Court deals with the issue relating to the cause of action and came to a conclusion that it would be pertinent to mention here that the term cause of action represents all the requisites and the facts which are necessary for the plaintiff to prove, before he can succeed in a suit. In the case reported in 53 DLR (AD) 12 their Lordships came to a conclusion that if the ultimate result of the suit is as clear as day light such a suit should be buried at its inception So that no further time is consumed in a fruitless litigation. In the case reported in 51 DLR (AD) 155 their Lordships came to a Conclusion that in a partition suit in the hotchpotch the property are to be attracted in an appropriate manner. In the case reported in 49 DLR (AD) 151 their Lordships came to a conclusion that all the properties should be included in a suit for partition. A partial partition can be allowed, where it is not proved that the parties Will be prejudiced by such partition. In the case reported in 54 DLR 310, the Court came to a conclusion that it is not the relief which is claimed, rather it is the relief which can be given to the plaintiff, that has to be looked into and considered in a plaint. In the Case reported in 22 BLD 423 = 54 DLR 310 the Court came to a conclusion, where certain facts are brought to the notice of the Court, which is not mentioned in the plaint or which arises, subsequent to filing of the suit and accepted as true by both the parties, such a fact can be taken into consideration in considering rejecting of the plaint under Order VII, rule 11 of the Code. In a case reported in 2002 SCMR 338 (Supreme Court Of Pakistan) the Court came to a conclusion besides the averments made in the plaint, other material available on record which on its own strength was legally sufficient can be looked into for the purpose of rejection, of the plaint.

22. We find no reason to deviate ourselves from the decisions as referred by the learned Advocate for the respondent but at the same time it is apparent that all the aforesaid decisions except few relates to adjudication of suit on merit. The aforesaid principles as laid down by their Lordships of this Court as well as of our Apex Court relates to issues which were determined by the Court below after framing issues and determination of the same in accordance with law. The pertinent question in the present case in hand is that whether a plaint is liable to be rejected or not.

23. We have mentioned earlier about the legal scope relating to rejection of plaints which attracts Order VII, rule 11 read with section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure and also the proposition as decided by this Court as well as of our Apex Court. It is admitted that the Court reserved the absolute power to reject plaint but at the same time it is desirable that while rejecting a plaint the Court should not travel beyond the plaint in any manner. Apart from that if the Court of law interest to exercise its power under section 151 of the Code in that case it is to be apparent in a crystal clear manner to the Court that further proceeding of a suit is a fruit-less litigation. Apart from that a plaint is to be considered in its entirety. It appears from the plaint in hand that the plaintiffs stated as follows:

অত্র মোকদ্দমা কারন বিগত ১০-১-২০০৮ ইং তারিখে ১নং বিবাদী যখন নালিশী সম��পত্তি ভাগবন্টন করিয়া দিতে অস্বীকার করেন অতপর বিগত ২১-১-২০০৮ ইং তারিখে বাদী যখন ‘ক’ তফসিলে বর্নিত নালিশী দলিলের সই মুহুরী নকল পাইয়া জানিতে ও বুঝিতে পারেনা তখন অত্র আদালতের এখতিয়ারধীন মতিঝিল থানা এলাকাধীন নালিশী সম্পত্তিতে উদ্ভব হইয়া নিত্যই বিদ্যমান রহিয়াছে।

24. So it appears that the plaintiffs raises certain dates regarding the cause of action.

25. The question of limitation involved determination of fact and law which requires investigation and on such plea of limitation a plaint should not be rejected unless it is so clear and palpable from a meaningful reading of the plaint that no further evidence is required. It is apparent of the face of the record of the case in hand that the plaintiffs stated certain dates with narration of their knowledge of the cause of action. It is admitted that the plaintiffs in the present case in hand challenges an old deed but in the plaint they have given a detailed description about the delay in an appropriate manner. It is up to the discretion of they Court to consider it or not but the same can only be done after framing issues and taking evidence on that point. It is the absolute freedom and liberty of the trial Court to decide the question of limitation and doing so, when certain factual aspects are involved in that case it is desirable that a Court of law should frame issues and dispose of the question of limitation. The question of cause of action is not an isolated one. Cause of action is a bundle of facts and it is to be revealed from the plaint upon its entirely. A cause of action cannot be isolated from the plaint or the pleadings rather a cause of action is to be considered on the given facts and circumstances. When a cause of action is surrounded in the plaint in different ways and in that case it can only be stated that the same relates to certain facts which attract the cause of action and ultimately the cause of action is to be considered by taking evidence and for which a specific issue is to be framed. As quoted by us earlier that while rejecting the plaint the Court below came to a conclusion that the predecessor of the defendant, for depriving the plain-tiffs, made the Heba which cannot be accept-able. Apart from that the Court below came to a conclusion that person can donate her entire property depriving all the other heirs. So it appears that the Court below in the present case in hand not only travelled beyond the plaint but also determined certain factual issues regarding the genuineness of the deed in question. Apart from that the Court below came to a conclusion that though the deed was registered in a different district but on that ground the same cannot be looked into a suspicious manner. While deciding the application under Order VII, rule 11 of the Code the Court below came to a conclusion regarding the knowledge in the following manner; কাজেই বাদী পক্ষেও এই দাবিটি আপাতত যুক্তিগ্রহ ও প্রমাণিত নয়।

<u>26. While dealings with an application for rejection of plaint the question of prove on disprove cannot play any role because as per the civil jurisprudence the question of prove and disprove are totally based upon evidence which can only be done after framing issues and taking evidence. While rejecting plaint the Court below also came to conclusion regarding the schedule of the property which is absolutely a question of fact.</u>

27. Considering the facts and circumstances, we are of the view that in the present case in hand the Court below travelled beyond the plaint and considered numerous materials which can only be done after framing issues and taking evidence, as such we are of the view that the Court below committed an illegality in rejecting the plaint.

28. Considering the facts and circumstances the appeal is allowed. The judgment and decree passed by the Court below is hereby set-aside. Consequently, the connected Rules are disposed herewith in the following direction and observations. That the Rule relate to an order of injunction which is disposed here with and the order of status quo granted by this Court shall continue for a period of 3(three) months from the date of receipt of this order by the Court below. In the meantime, the plaintiffs are at liberty to file an application, for injunction if so advised.

29. The rule so far relates to violation is also disposed of as the same can only be decided by taking evidence.

30. The Court below is directed to dispose of the suit expeditiously as possible. Communicate this order at once.

End.
